<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!--
      Singular TargetFramework, and deliberately so. A Native AOT publish produces one native
      binary for one framework and one RID; a multi-targeted probe would either need -f on every
      publish or would silently probe whichever framework the tooling picked. The value is still
      $(LibraryTargetFrameworks) rather than a literal, so this project cannot drift away from
      what the packages target — and if that property ever becomes a list again, MSBuild rejects
      'net10.0;net11.0' as a TargetFramework by name, which is a loud failure rather than a quiet
      half-probe.
    -->
    <TargetFramework>$(LibraryTargetFrameworks)</TargetFramework>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <RootNamespace>DatabentoDotNet.AotProbe</RootNamespace>

    <!--
      The point of the project. PublishAot turns on the ILC compiler at publish time and, with it,
      EnableTrimAnalyzer and EnableAotAnalyzer for this project and everything it compiles — so an
      IL2xxx/IL3xxx the four packages' own analysis would have caught statically is caught here
      too, and the ones only ILC can see are caught when it runs.
    -->
    <PublishAot>true</PublishAot>

    <!--
      TrimmerSingleWarn=false makes the trimmer report every warning rather than collapsing an
      assembly's worth into one "AssemblyName has trim warnings" line. The DoD for #64 is *zero*
      warnings, and a single collapsed line is the shape that hides how many there really are.
    -->
    <TrimmerSingleWarn>false</TrimmerSingleWarn>

    <!--
      Not packable, not a test project — the two properties benchmarks/DatabentoDotNet.Benchmarks
      already documents, for the same two reasons: `dotnet pack` at the root must not produce a
      DatabentoDotNet.AotProbe package, and `dotnet test` must not try to run an assembly with no
      tests in it. Explicitly false rather than merely unset, because Directory.Build.targets reads
      IsTestProject and an empty value works by accident.

      IsProbeProject is read there too, and excludes this project from $(ShippingProject) — see
      that file for why the exclusion is about the public API lock rather than about AOT analysis.
    -->
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <IsTestProject>false</IsTestProject>
    <IsProbeProject>true</IsProbeProject>

    <!-- MockLiveGateway, compiled below, reinterprets record structs the same way the codec does. -->
    <AllowUnsafeBlocks>true</AllowUnsafeBlocks>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    All four packages, because "the AOT publish succeeds" is a claim about all four and ILC only
    compiles what it can reach. A reference nothing calls is trimmed away entirely and proves
    nothing, so every probe in Program.cs exists to *reach* into one of these.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="../../src/DatabentoDotNet.Dbn/DatabentoDotNet.Dbn.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="../../src/DatabentoDotNet.Live/DatabentoDotNet.Live.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="../../src/DatabentoDotNet.Historical/DatabentoDotNet.Historical.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="../../src/DatabentoDotNet.Reference/DatabentoDotNet.Reference.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    MockLiveGateway compresses, so the probe needs the zstd encoder for the same reason the Live
    test project does: the library only ever decompresses, and adding an unused compression seam to
    it so a harness could call one would work against CLAUDE.md's one-file rule rather than with it.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="ZstdSharp.Port" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Source compiled from the test projects rather than copied here. This is CLAUDE.md's
    one-file-two-projects arrangement — the same one DatabentoDotNet.Live uses for
    Internal/ZstdDecompressor.cs — applied for the same reason: a second copy is a second thing to
    keep correct, and the whole value of the probe is that it reaches the *same* answers as the
    managed test suite. A copy that drifted would still report success.

    ExpectedRecordCounts is the decoder's independent oracle: 71 fixture names to the record counts
    upstream's dbn CLI reports. DbnDecoderTests asserts against it under the managed runtime and
    this probe asserts against it inside a native binary, which is only a comparison while there is
    one table.

    The four gateway files are MockLiveGateway and what it needs. Writing a smaller loopback gateway
    for the probe would be a third implementation of the live protocol after the mock and the real
    one, which is exactly the thing CLAUDE.md's testing section argues against: two implementations
    written from the same reading agree with each other, and a third only widens that. Linking costs
    nothing and cannot drift.

    A test project reference would have been the obvious alternative and is the wrong one: it drags
    xunit and Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk into a Native AOT publish, neither of which is trim-safe and
    neither of which the probe has any use for. None of the four files below mentions xunit.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <Compile Include="../../tests/DatabentoDotNet.Dbn.Tests/ExpectedRecordCounts.cs" Link="Linked/ExpectedRecordCounts.cs" />
    <Compile Include="../../tests/DatabentoDotNet.Live.Tests/MockLiveGateway.cs" Link="Linked/MockLiveGateway.cs" />
    <Compile Include="../../tests/DatabentoDotNet.Live.Tests/GatewayLineReader.cs" Link="Linked/GatewayLineReader.cs" />
    <Compile Include="../../tests/DatabentoDotNet.Live.Tests/MockGatewayException.cs" Link="Linked/MockGatewayException.cs" />
    <Compile Include="../../tests/DatabentoDotNet.Live.Tests/ExpectedSubscription.cs" Link="Linked/ExpectedSubscription.cs" />
    <Compile Include="../../tests/DatabentoDotNet.Live.Tests/SyntheticMbo.cs" Link="Linked/SyntheticMbo.cs" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The same vendored corpus the decoder tests read, copied beside the native binary so the probe is
    offline and self-contained: no credentials, no network, runnable anywhere the binary is.

    Four glob patterns for the same reason DatabentoDotNet.Dbn.Tests uses four: "*.dbn" matches only
    names literally ending in .dbn, not the .zst / .frag / .frag.zst variants. The probe checks the
    files on disk against ExpectedRecordCounts' 71 keys before decoding anything, so a broken glob
    fails as a missing fixture rather than as a smaller corpus quietly reporting success.

    The reference fixture is the 879 KB corporate_actions.list_enums response #58 captured. It is
    here to be served over loopback to the real ReferenceClient, which is the only way to reach the
    source-generated JSON contexts — those are internal, so nothing else can call them.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <Content Include="../../tests/DatabentoDotNet.Dbn.Tests/Data/*.dbn" Link="Data/%(Filename)%(Extension)" CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest" />
    <Content Include="../../tests/DatabentoDotNet.Dbn.Tests/Data/*.dbn.zst" Link="Data/%(Filename)%(Extension)" CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest" />
    <Content Include="../../tests/DatabentoDotNet.Dbn.Tests/Data/*.dbn.frag" Link="Data/%(Filename)%(Extension)" CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest" />
    <Content Include="../../tests/DatabentoDotNet.Dbn.Tests/Data/*.dbn.frag.zst" Link="Data/%(Filename)%(Extension)" CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest" />
    <Content Include="../../tests/DatabentoDotNet.Reference.Tests/Data/corporate_actions.list_enums.json" Link="Data/corporate_actions.list_enums.json" CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
